DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       The  ooppeenn__ttrreeee()  system  call is part of the suite of file‐descriptor‐
       based mount facilities in Linux.

       •  If _f_l_a_g_s contains OOPPEENN__TTRREEEE__CCLLOONNEE, ooppeenn__ttrreeee()  creates  a  detached
          mount object which consists of a bind‐mount of the path specified by
          the  _p_a_t_h.  A new file descriptor associated with the detached mount
          object is then returned.  The mount object is equivalent to a  bind‐
          mount  that would be created by mmoouunntt(2) called with MMSS__BBIINNDD, except
          that it is tied to a file descriptor and is  not  mounted  onto  the
          filesystem.

          As  with  file  descriptors  returned from ffssmmoouunntt(2), the resultant
          file descriptor can  then  be  used  with  mmoovvee__mmoouunntt(2),  mmoouunntt__ssee‐‐
          ttaattttrr(2), or other such system calls to do further mount operations.

          This  mount object will be unmounted and destroyed when the file de‐
          scriptor is closed if it was not otherwise attached to a mount point
          by calling mmoovvee__mmoouunntt(2).  This implicit unmount operation is  lazy—
          akin  to calling uummoouunntt22(2) with MMNNTT__DDEETTAACCHH; thus, any existing open
          references to files from the mount object will continue to work, and
          the mount object will only be completely destroyed once it ceases to
          be busy.

       •  If _f_l_a_g_s does not contain  OOPPEENN__TTRREEEE__CCLLOONNEE,  ooppeenn__ttrreeee()  returns  a
          file  descriptor  that is exactly equivalent to one produced by ooppee‐‐
          nnaatt(2) when called with the same _d_i_r_f_d and _p_a_t_h.

       In either case, the resultant file descriptor acts the same as one pro‐
       duced by ooppeenn(2) with OO__PPAATTHH, meaning it can also be used  as  a  _d_i_r_f_d
       argument  to "*at()" system calls.  However, unlike ooppeenn(2) called with
       OO__PPAATTHH, automounts will by default be triggered by  ooppeenn__ttrreeee()  unless
       AATT__NNOO__AAUUTTOOMMOOUUNNTT is included in _f_l_a_g_s.

       As  with  "*at()"  system calls, ooppeenn__ttrreeee() uses the _d_i_r_f_d argument in
       conjunction with the _p_a_t_h argument to determine the path to operate on,
       as follows:

       •  If the pathname given in _p_a_t_h is absolute, then _d_i_r_f_d is ignored.

       •  If the pathname given in _p_a_t_h is relative and _d_i_r_f_d is  the  special
          value  AATT__FFDDCCWWDD,  then  _p_a_t_h  is interpreted relative to the current
          working directory of the calling process (like ooppeenn(2)).

       •  If the pathname given in _p_a_t_h is relative, then  it  is  interpreted
          relative  to  the directory referred to by the file descriptor _d_i_r_f_d
          (rather than relative to the current working directory of the  call‐
          ing  process,  as  is  done by ooppeenn(2) for a relative pathname).  In
          this case, _d_i_r_f_d must be a directory that was opened for reading  (‐
          OO__RRDDOONNLLYY) or using the OO__PPAATTHH flag.

       •  If  _p_a_t_h  is an empty string, and _f_l_a_g_s contains AATT__EEMMPPTTYY__PPAATTHH, then
          the file descriptor _d_i_r_f_d is operated on directly.   In  this  case,
          _d_i_r_f_d may refer to any type of file, not just a directory.

